notes.p:
Predict the output, then check:

`#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    int a = 5;
    int *p = &a;
    *p = *p * 2;
    printf("%d\n", a);
    printf("%d\n", 10 >> 1 | 1);
    char str[] = "HCL";
    printf("%zu %zu\n", sizeof(str), strlen(str));
    int i, s = 0;
    for (i = 1; i <= 10; i += 3) s += i;
    printf("%d %d\n", s, i);
    return 0;
}`

Output:
`10
5
4 3
22 13`

Line by line: `*p` changes `a` to 10. Shift binds tighter than `|`, so (10 >> 1) | 1 = 5 | 1 = 5. `sizeof` counts the hidden `\0`, `strlen` does not. The loop adds 1 + 4 + 7 + 10 = 22 and stops when i becomes 13.

